X-MARC-Message: https://marc.info/?l=tru64-unix-managers&m=102653731622890

To add to my woes with a DS20, the machine crashed with the following
symptoms

1.  An ADVFS file system ran out of inodes (despite df -i reporting the
    file system not full for I-nodes)
2.  The kernel kept deffering i/o to the disk
3.  All the other file systems then ran out of i-nodes (again df -i did
    not say this but the kern.log did.  I do not have the kern.log as
    something has erased it after the reboot)

as a result of 3 the system became unusable and I could not even shut it
down gracefully and had to hit the power off.

It is running tru64 5.0 it is not patched upto date (I am preparing to do
that now...).

Does any body recognoise these symptoms?

Any help would be appreciated.



From tru64-unix-managers  Wed Mar 27 13:38:27 2002
From: jreed () athena ! appliedtheory ! com
Date: Wed, 27 Mar 2002 13:38:27 +0000
To: tru64-unix-managers
Subject: SUMMARY: migrating boot disks
X-MARC-Message: https://marc.info/?l=tru64-unix-managers&m=102653731622889

Thanks to all who responded so quickly. Consensus seems
to be that plan will generally work, but vdump/vrestore to new
OS disks while system is up will give me a better copy and prevent
possible problems with restoring from a backup that didn't know
about new LSM config.

Other items were:
	* may not need to take system down if disks are hot-pluggable
	* /etc/passwd, /var/tcb, /var/... files might change =

		during vdump/vrestore
	* "consvar" could be used to change console params - I've crashed
		a system or two, though, using consvar, so am wary!
	* if a cluster, could be very complicated, may need to remove
		a cluster member and re-add to recreate its boot disk.
	* make sure to modify /etc/rc.config swap partition reference.
	* V5.* makes the whole exercise much more complicated!

John Losey had an interesting scenario where you keep the OS disks at sam=
e
locations by swapping old with new and then booting CD and using =

vdump/vrestore,
here are details:
	--------------------------------------------------------
One thought might be to: (Assuming 4.0x, I'm not overly sure how 5.x woul=
d =

handle something similar)
*=A0 Take the system down. =

*=A0 Move the two old disks to the empty slots. =

*=A0 Put the new disks into the newly available slots. (this would keep L=
SM & =

/etc/fstab happier).
*=A0 Boot to the OS CD, go to shell mode. =

*=A0 Partition new disks. =

*=A0 Mount new disk & old disk (only need to mount the primaries, since y=
ou =

implied they are LSM mirrors).
*=A0 Use vdump | vrestore (syntax near the bottom of the manpage for vres=
tore) =

to dump from old OS disk to new OS disk.
*=A0 Shutdown the system. =

*=A0 Boot to the new disk (which should be the same console setting as it=
 was =

before)
Since LSM is expecting your / & /usr part of the LSM Mirror to be at a =

specific disk (say /dev/rz0), by putting the new disks at that location, =

you'll keep LSM happy while still migrating to new disks.=A0 If you're =

using Tru64 5.x, things get a bit different because the old disks =

would probably still be /dev/disk/dsk0 (I believe it tracks the serial# =

of the drive and would notice that the old drives were at a new Target =

and keep the old name).=A0 Though there have been posts lately talking =

about how to clear out the device information, so this might be able =

to be worked around as well.
	-------------------------------------------------------------
alan@nabeth notes that in V4 moving the physical system disk is easy, =

but in V5 OS remembers what disk it was on and makes things more complica=
ted.
He also notes that there should be summaries about the v5 issue
of modifying the hardware database, and that there might be a best
practices paper on Compaq's website.
	--------------------------------------------------------------
Several people sent more or less detailed instructions, you know who
you are, huge thanks to you!!!!!!!

Regards,
Judith Reed


