Thanks to Timothy Brown, Dr. Thomas Blinn, Jay Leafey and Raul Sossa for
your responses.
 
Timothy and Raul suggested updating the firmware, which is a good idea
regardless and has been done.
Dr. Blinn and Jay both correctly identified the problem as an ECU
configuration error.  Below is Dr. Blinn's summary of the problem and
solution:
 
Your system is clearly set up to use a serial console, and the kernel
would like to use a serial console, but it isn't finding the serial line
hardware:
 
> pci0 (primary bus:0) at mcbus0 slot 4
> eisa0 at pci0
 
and then nothing.  In the console, do a "show config" and if you can
pipe it to more, do so.  You are looking for whether the console can
find and report the serial line devices.  I'm guessing that the console
is seeing them just find, but that your EISA configuration data is hosed
for some reason.  Find your ECU diskette (EISA configuration utility)
and re-run it and save the configuration data.  That should be all that
you need.  Or try running with the graphics console, assuming you have
the hardware to do that.
 
(Strictly speaking, it's not "NOTHING" after it sees the EISA bus
interface, it reports other PCI options, but it doesn't report anything
on the EISA bus, and what's reported depends on the EISA configuration
data which is exported by firmware 
to the OS for use in system configuration.)
 
Here is the root problem:
 
> configure_console: No serial driver from get_info
> No kernel console driver installed
 
but the console does seem to know about the serial line:
 
> com1_baud               9600
> com1_flow               SOFTWARE
> com1_modem              OFF
> com2_baud               9600
> com2_flow               SOFTWARE
> com2_modem              OFF
> console                 serial
 
 
 
