Greetings all,

I am in the planning stage to upgrade from 4.0f -> 5.0a -> 5.1a.

The environment:  2 x ds40 w/ba370 over fcal in a cluster configuration with
ORACLE 8.1.6/11i.

Our end state is to dissolve the cluster (not needed), upgrade the O/S
(twice), and apply patches as required.

THE QUESTION:  How can I remove the cluster without doing a fresh install of
the O/S?

References to doc sources are welcome, I couldn't find any thing on the
tru64 web site.

Thanks in advance.

Mark

From tru64-unix-managers  Tue Mar 19 17:07:12 2002
From: dr john halewood <john () frumious ! unidec ! co ! uk>
Date: Tue, 19 Mar 2002 17:07:12 +0000
To: tru64-unix-managers
Subject: Console bell volume on PW 433a
X-MARC-Message: https://marc.info/?l=tru64-unix-managers&m=102653731622784

Hi,
  A client of ours has an issue with a Personal Workstation 433a (running 
DU4.0d, unfortunately) which has left me confused. The system runs some 
monitoring software and is designed to alert users by beeping, using the 
console bell, when an incident occurs. However the console bell on the system 
is so quiet it's barely audible above the noise of the fans. The system does 
not by default run X, so xset b (the obvious solution) has no effect. Does 
anyone know how to set the console bell volume outside of an X session?
 Some additional information that might be useful: after starting X, I still 
can't get any more volume from the internal speaker. I've also tried 
disabling the onboard CS42XX soundcard (made no difference) and attaching 
headphones and speakers (they cut off the internal speaker but generate 
virtually no sound).
 I suspect there might be a hardware problem but would be grateful for any 
suggestions anyone has.

cheers
john

From tru64-unix-managers  Tue Mar 19 16:38:11 2002
From: fybar <fybar () powersurfr ! com>
Date: Tue, 19 Mar 2002 16:38:11 +0000
To: tru64-unix-managers
Subject: Summary: restore /usr/bin
X-MARC-Message: https://marc.info/?l=tru64-unix-managers&m=102653731622783

Thanks to alan@nabeth, Warren Sturm, Tom Blinn, Elizabeth Harvey and
Prithwish Chatterjee for responding.  I tried two methods, both of which
worked.  I copied the bin directory from the installation media cdrom and I
did an install on a spare disk and copied the bin directory over to the
fractured disk after mounting it.  One caveat, su came up without the setuid
bit set, so I could su to other users from root, but not to root from users
in the system group.  After I set the bit, of course, it worked.  I don't
know if there are any other gotchas in store, but I am going to restore from
a backup as soon as I get my new tape drive, for now it is functional.

Original post below:

> A student accidently removed /usr/bin, or most of it anyways, trying to
> update symlinks for a new version of JDK.(don't ask)  The tape drive that
> was used for the backups is kaput as of last weekend.(perfect timing)  Is
> there a way to recover the /usr/bin executables that came with the
original
> installation from the installation media?  I just want to insure a
> reasonable level of functionality until the recovery from tape can be
made.
>

fybar

