X-MARC-Message: https://marc.info/?l=tru64-unix-managers&m=102653731622763


To keep things short, the OS doesn't do what it should.

The more technical explanation courtesy of Dr Blinn:
The KZPCM-DA card contains a PCI-to-PCI bridge. 'So when you move it to a 
different slot, you've just moved a whole PCI bus hierarchy within your 
kernel -- and I suspect that it is that action, combined with some kind 
of logic in the device naming persistency code that's not working 
correctly for this case, that breaks your kernel and your Ethernet 
naming.'

To keep things neat, I'll probably reinstall from scratch, to make sure 
things get named OK.


Regards,

Rob

 -----Original Message-----

Hi,

We're in the process of moving to 5.1A but are hitting a few problems.

Looking through the archives, I believe that we should be able to move
cards between PCI slots, and the OS should detect where they are without
having to mess around with the kernel etc. However this doesn't appear to 

be happening.

We have two similar, but not identical DS20's, ie with the same cards but 

in different slots, one live on 4.0F, one testing for 5.1A.
The aim is to build a system disk on a fibre channel SAN, with 5.1A on
the test server, and then get the live server to boot off that disk, to
make the upgrade quicker.

If I install a new copy of 5.1A onto the test DS20. Everything works 
fine.
I then move the combined SCSI / ethernet card (KZPCM-DA) from PCI slot x
to PCI slot y.
On booting the ethernet adapter is not recognised.
So I've then booted from the generic kernel, and done a doconfig, however 

this then puts the ethernet adapter onto tu1 rather than tu0.
I could reconfigure the network to be on tu1 rather than tu0, but I don't 

believe I should need to do this. Surely the OS should be recognising
that this is the first ethernet adapter and name it properly as tu0, or
am I missing something ?


