  10.0pt;font-family:Arial'>Andy</span></font><font face=Arial><span
 lang=EN-GB style='font-family:Arial'> </span></font><font face=Arial><span
  lang=EN-GB style='font-family:Arial'>Cohen</span></font><font face=Arial><span
lang=EN-GB style='font-family:Arial'> for</span></font></p>

<p class=MsoNormal><font size=2 color=blue face="Comic Sans MS"><span
lang=EN-GB style='font-size:10.0pt;font-family:"Comic Sans MS";color:blue'>I
agree with the need to defrag periodically (what db are you using?&nbsp;I've
heard it said that Oracle doesn't cause&nbsp;fragmentation).&nbsp; My concern
would be rebooting with a script -- what happens if it shuts down but doesn't
restart successfully?&nbsp; That's happened to me and I've had to come in and
push the power button.&nbsp; There is hardware/software that can be
added/configured to let you do a cold boot (power cycle) remotely.&nbsp; This
may be worth looking into.</span></font></p>

<p class=MsoNormal><font size=2 color=blue face="Comic Sans MS"><span
lang=EN-GB style='font-size:10.0pt;font-family:"Comic Sans MS";color:blue'>&nbsp;</span></font></p>

<p class=MsoNormal><font size=2 color=blue face="Comic Sans MS"><span
  lang=EN-GB style='font-size:10.0pt;font-family:"Comic Sans MS";color:blue'>Colin</span></font><font
 color=blue face="Comic Sans MS"><span lang=EN-GB style='font-family:"Comic Sans MS";
 color:blue'> </span></font><font color=blue face="Comic Sans MS"><span
  lang=EN-GB style='font-family:"Comic Sans MS";color:blue'>Bull</span></font><font
color=blue face="Comic Sans MS"><span lang=EN-GB style='font-family:"Comic Sans MS";
color:blue'> for</span></font></p>

<p class=MsoNormal><font size=2 color=blue face=Arial><span lang=EN-GB
style='font-size:10.0pt;font-family:Arial;color:blue'>I guess if your databse
is any good, and you allocate space correctly, it is very rare that you need to
defrag your disk at all for databases. Both Oracle and Informix let you use raw
disk, which can get over this problem completely if you administer the tables
properly.</span></font></p>

<p class=MsoNormal><font size=2 color=blue face=Arial><span lang=EN-GB
style='font-size:10.0pt;font-family:Arial;color:blue'>&nbsp;</span></font></p>

<p class=MsoNormal><font size=2 color=blue face=Arial><span lang=EN-GB
  style='font-size:10.0pt;font-family:Arial;color:blue'>Mike</span></font><font
 color=blue face=Arial><span lang=EN-GB style='font-family:Arial;color:blue'> </span></font><font
  color=blue face=Arial><span lang=EN-GB style='font-family:Arial;color:blue'>Deacy</span></font></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-autospace:none'><font size=2 face="Courier New"><span
style='font-size:10.0pt;font-family:"Courier New"'>Seems like a good plan to
me.&nbsp; I suppose you can try it and see how this </span></font></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-autospace:none'><font size=2 face="Courier New"><span
style='font-size:10.0pt;font-family:"Courier New"'>schedule suits your
fragmentation needs.&nbsp; I would also expect that </span></font></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-autospace:none'><font size=2 face="Courier New"><span
style='font-size:10.0pt;font-family:"Courier New"'>maintenance type operations
are best done when a system is less busy.&nbsp; I'm </span></font></p>

<p class=MsoNormal><font size=2 face="Courier New"><span style='font-size:10.0pt;
font-family:"Courier New"'>sure they'll complete faster and also not impact any
production activity.</span></font></p>

<p class=MsoNormal><font size=2 face=Arial><span lang=EN-GB style='font-size:
10.0pt;font-family:Arial'>&nbsp;</span></font></p>

<p class=MsoNormal><font size=2 face=Arial><span lang=EN-GB style='font-size:
10.0pt;font-family:Arial'>&nbsp;</span></font></p>

<p class=MsoNormal><font size=2 face="Times New Roman"><span lang=EN-GB
style='font-size:10.0pt'>&nbsp;</span></font></p>

<p><font size=2 face=Tahoma><span style='font-size:10.0pt;font-family:Tahoma'>Kind
regards</span></font> </p>

<p><font size=2 face=Tahoma><span style='font-size:10.0pt;font-family:Tahoma'>Lawrie</span></font><font
 size=2 face=Tahoma><span style='font-size:10.0pt;font-family:Tahoma'> </span></font><font
  size=2 face=Tahoma><span style='font-size:10.0pt;font-family:Tahoma'>Smith</span></font>
