The way I understand to protocal, the time shifts are not large jumps =
but=20
instead small changes for a period of time to match up the times.  Plus=20
once the times are in sync, the daemon checks quite often for changes =
and=20
so only needs to make small changes.  When a system first boots (or you=20
start xntp via /sbin/init.d/xntpd start) it first runs ntpdate which =
will=20
do a large jump.

if the two dates are to far out of range, xnpt will not try to sync.  =
this=20
helps avoid the issue you fear below.

What I would do is check how far apart the two dates are (xntp server =
and=20
local system) and adjust the time manually to be within a couple of=20
seconds.  Then I would start xntpd using the init.d startup command or=20
using sysman.

And you can do this off hours (non-peak) without issues.


